package cosmonic:cron@0.1.0;

/// Managing the schedules that call the caller's workload.
///
/// Importing this grants one power: the caller may decide when its own
/// workload's `handler` is called. It cannot schedule another workload, and it
/// cannot name what gets called, because there is no parameter for either. A
/// firing always lands on the `handler` export of the workload that registered
/// the schedule.
///
/// A workload whose schedules are known when it is deployed does not need this
/// interface at all. Those are declared with the workload and it only exports
/// `handler`. Import `scheduler` when the set of schedules is decided at run
/// time: a poll that starts when a user asks for it and stops when its work is
/// done.
///
/// ## Registrations outlive the caller
///
/// A component instance may exist only for the length of one call, so a
/// registration is held by the implementer under its name rather than by a
/// handle the caller would have to keep alive.
///
/// ## How a registration ends
///
/// - `unregister` removes it.
/// - A schedule that fires once is removed when its firing has been handled,
///   or given up on.
/// - The implementer may end it. Whoever grants this interface can bound how
///   long a registration is held and how many times it fires, so that a
///   schedule its component forgot does not run forever. A registration ended
///   this way simply stops; nothing is delivered to say so.
/// - The caller's workload stops.
///
/// Whether a registration survives a restart of the implementer is also up to
/// the implementer. In every case `list-registrations` is how a caller finds
/// out what is still held, and registering again is how it restores what is
/// not.
@since(version = 0.1.0)
interface scheduler {
    @since(version = 0.1.0)
    use types.{schedule-name, schedule, registration, schedule-error};

    /// Start calling the caller's workload on `schedule`, under `name`.
    ///
    /// Registering a name that is already registered replaces its schedule,
    /// and the new one takes effect from now: an `interval` starts a fresh
    /// period. A firing of the old schedule that is already being handled is
    /// allowed to finish.
    ///
    /// Registering a name with exactly the schedule it already has changes
    /// nothing: the phase of an `interval` is kept, a pending `once-after`
    /// keeps counting from when it was first registered, and any bound the
    /// implementer puts on the registration's lifetime is not extended. A
    /// component can therefore call this every time it starts, without
    /// tracking whether it has done so before.
    ///
    /// The implementer validates the schedule before returning, so an `ok`
    /// means it will fire.
    @since(version = 0.1.0)
    register: async func(name: schedule-name, schedule: schedule)
        -> result<_, schedule-error>;

    /// Stop calling the caller's workload for `name`.
    ///
    /// Removing a name that is not registered succeeds, so the call is safe to
    /// repeat. A firing already being handled is allowed to finish and is not
    /// redelivered.
    @since(version = 0.1.0)
    unregister: async func(name: schedule-name) -> result<_, schedule-error>;

    /// Every schedule held for the caller's workload, ordered by name: the
    /// ones it registered and the ones declared for it.
    ///
    /// Never lists another workload's schedules.
    @since(version = 0.1.0)
    list-registrations: async func() -> result<list<registration>, schedule-error>;
}
